From: Adam Hillson <598804+BadAd84@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] server: render and tokenize a request once, and TF_GLM_OVN_HOST_ONCE

Fork ai-engine, ref fork-ssh/ovn-up, commit ef81b4a49 "server: render and
tokenize a request once; TF_GLM_OVN_HOST_ONCE", the rest of it. Patch 01 took
its two standalone optimisations and patch 02 its bench.

What it was. prompt.prepare rendered and tokenized the whole conversation up
to four times for one request: the prompt, the history, the continued-turn
probe and the rewind probe, each a full template render and a full masked
encode, and each render parsed every tool call again. On a long agent turn
that is the engine thread's single largest piece of per-request host work.

What it is now. The prompt is rendered once and its ids are kept with the
added-token segments they came from (core/tokenizer/kept.zig). Each probe
renders its own text, but encodes only the segments that differ from the
prompt's: the leading and trailing runs are the prompt's own ids, taken by
pointer. The history probe usually needs no render at all, because the
template says where its own text ends: a final `{% if add_generation_prompt %}`
block that nothing else reads is found at compile time
(core/template/generation.zig) and the generation-on render records the length
before it, so the history is that prefix of the prompt's text. Tool-call
arguments are parsed once a request and reused by every later render of the
same message (messages.Args).

TF_GLM_OVN_HOST_ONCE=1, default off, adds one more step: the rewind probe's
render is watched against the prompt's text (core/template/watch.zig) and
stops a few KiB past the first byte where they differ, because by then the
answer to "where do the two id streams part" is already fixed. The knob is
read once at start-up (server.host_once).

The ids must not move, and that is what the tests check. kept_test runs
random masked texts and random edits of them against a fresh encodeMasked,
3,000 rounds each, and asserts the reuse actually happens; generation_test
runs 200 random chats through the GLM-shaped template and asserts the
flag-off render is the flag-on render up to the mark, spans included, plus
400 watched renders that are prefixes of the full one; prompt_once_test
drives the real prepare over random conversations against a reference that
is the old four-render path, comparing ids, history_len, rewind_len and
user_end, with the knob off and on. All of them run under test-cuda-host, and
the fork's own `test-prompt-once-host` step runs the three sets alone.

No patch in this stack touches these files, so this completes nothing earlier;
it completes patches 01 and 02 of this lane.

What is not the fork's own lines, and why. The fork wrote this against a base
140 upstream commits older than our pin, and prompt.zig is where that shows:

- the pin's renderIds is a thin call to renderRequest, which carries a Kimi
  bypass, a native-DeepSeek bypass, tool_choice, response_format and images
  render options, MiniMax-M3's own closing token, the templateClosed test and
  the explicit-thinking-off refusal. None of that exists in the fork's base.
  The fork's shape is ported onto it: renderText returns the model_text
  render (the pin's private render and RenderedIds go, since both the old path
  and Once want the Rendered itself), closer returns the one id a thinking-off
  generation prompt ends with and carries both of the pin's cases in the pin's
  order, and offRefusal is the pin's refusal lifted out so the prompt render
  inside Once runs it too. Once.prompt therefore takes a require_off argument
  the fork's version has no notion of.
- renderText is the fork's `rendered`, renamed: the pin's templateClosed takes
  a parameter called `rendered` and Zig refuses the shadow. Nothing else about
  it changed except the pin's own options and the pin's video marker
  replacement, which it keeps.
- every scratch Cx a probe runs under keeps carrying cx.template_fields, which
  the pin added and the fork's base has no field for.
- model_text.Rendered.keep also falls back to a whole encode when the render
  carries Kimi release segments. Our Rendered has a `segments` field the fork's
  has not, and with segments present `encode` is a sequence of per-segment
  encodes, not encodeMasked(text, spans), so a kept state over it would be
  wrong. The fallback leaves `state` null, which is what makes pieces and
  prefixPieces take their own null-state paths; they need no segment test of
  their own.
- the fork registers `test-prompt-once-host` in zig/build/cuda.zig. Here the
  server host steps live in zig/build/cuda_server_host.zig, where server_tests,
  mods.tokenizer and the template module are all in scope, so the step is
  registered there, unchanged otherwise.
- the fork's keep, pieces, prefix and masked hooks sit inside hf_text.zig,
  which is 600 lines here and would be 626 with them: over the lean rule's
  limit. They are in zig/src/server/hf_text_kept.zig instead, the same bodies
  with the tokenizer reached through one helper, and hf_text.zig's vtable
  points straight at them (597 lines).
- zig/tests/server/prompt_bench.zig gets the eight rows patch 02 removed back,
  the fork's; its render helper passes our srv.media() where the fork writes
  .of(srv.info.vision).

Nothing of the commit is left out.

Measured here, no GPU: prompt_bench, five rounds, ReleaseFast, x86_64 laptop,
the two trees run back to back on an otherwise idle machine. The model is
GLM-4.6's tokenizer with the GLM-5.3 chat template, whose every word is one of
that tokenizer's added tokens; the corpus is this tree's own zig sources.
Median chat.prepare, before then after:

  the parity probe's r2, 223,956 ids          3.54 -> 1.31 ms
  the same with template words quoted         4.03 -> 1.33 ms
  an agent turn of 215,115 ids on a tool      20.72 -> 11.57 ms
  an agent turn of 636,930 ids on a tool      63.35 -> 35.89 ms

Against a tokenizer whose added tokens the template does not write (DeepSeek
V3.2's pair, where every render is one long segment and only the history mark
and the memo help) the same r2 shape reads 387.33 -> 144.74 ms.

The prompt digest and all three marks are identical before and after in every
case, which prompt_bench itself asserts each round (IdsDiffer, MarksDiffer).

What a GPU run has to say. The host saving is measured above and needs no
GPU. What a serve has to show is that nothing moved underneath it: the same
reply digests as the stack through 0042 at 1, 2, 4, 6 and 8 streams with every
knob off, the same with TF_GLM_OVN_HOST_ONCE=1, and a long agent turn (the
650k shape) returning the pin's digest with the knob both ways. The marks the
engine receives decide prompt-cache reuse, so a run should also confirm that
history_len, rewind_len and user_end on a resumed conversation are what they
were, by the restore the serve reports rather than by the numbers alone.

Re-seated on v1.2.7 (93a2e93ecebff8aa52b88e58c5dd222516e69c5f, from pin 9c9bf542) by the 2026-11-12 re-seat (work/reseat/DECISIONS.md):
- lean_check.py's DIRECT_GETENV: server.zig read std.c.getenv("TF_GLM_OVN_HOST_ONCE") (any text but 1 off); it is now a .glm flag row read once by environment.value, so text other than 0 or 1 refuses the start.
- tokenizer.zig is 595 lines at v1.2.7 and the fold's hunks made it 615: Tokenizer.encodeSegments, the body the fold split out of encodeMaskedCounted, lives in kept.zig as encodeSegments(t, ...) beside its three callers, and the three Tokenizer helpers it calls (refuseAdded, encodeSegment, encodeTextMasked) are pub.
- zig/tests/server/prompt_bench.zig: the sites table comes back whole, as zig fmt aligns it (prompt-bench's own alignment differs since that patch's table is narrower).
- source: work/fold/0035-render-once-host-once.patch

